menu "Networking"

config NET_WIFI
    bool "Wi-Fi (station + SoftAP)"
    default y
    help
      Compiles the cyw43 station/AP state machine, the /api/wifi routes and
      the credential store in or out entirely. The Bluetooth stack shares the
      same CYW43439 but does not depend on this: a BLE-only build is valid and
      is reachable over USB-NCM.

config NET_USB_NCM
    bool "USB-NCM (Ethernet over the USB cable)"
    default y
    help
      A network interface over the target's own USB port. Worth keeping even
      on a Wi-Fi build: it is reachable before Wi-Fi is configured and when the
      access point is down, which is the difference between a bad Wi-Fi setting
      being an annoyance and being a reflash.

comment "USB-NCM /24: device takes .1, the host is handed .16 by DHCP."
comment "MUST NOT collide with another NCM device on the same host -- the bench"
comment "this was developed on already had an unrelated project on 192.168.7.x,"
comment "and two NCM interfaces sharing a subnet is a routing coin-toss."

config NET_NCM_IP0
    int "USB-NCM subnet octet 1"
    depends on NET_USB_NCM
    default 192

config NET_NCM_IP1
    int "USB-NCM subnet octet 2"
    depends on NET_USB_NCM
    default 168

config NET_NCM_IP2
    int "USB-NCM subnet octet 3"
    depends on NET_USB_NCM
    default 8

config NET_MDNS
    bool "mDNS responder"
    default y

config NET_HOSTNAME
    string "mDNS / DHCP hostname"
    default "bluetooth-rp2-webapp"
    help
      The project name, spelled as a DNS label: a hostname may contain only
      letters, digits and hyphens, so the underscores in "bluetooth_rp2_webapp"
      become hyphens. Reachable as <name>.local once mDNS is up.

config NET_HTTP_CLIENT
    bool "HTTP client for Lua scripts (http.start)"
    depends on LUA_ENABLE
    default y
    help
      An HTTP/1.1 client on raw lwIP TCP (src/net/httpc.c) for the Lua
      `http` module: DNS, Content-Length and chunked bodies, redirects,
      streaming into a LittleFS or SD file, timeouts, several requests at
      once. Turns lwIP's DNS client on. Requests and their buffers live in
      the Lua arena, not in static RAM.

config NET_HTTP_TLS
    bool "HTTPS (mbedTLS, TLS 1.2 client)"
    depends on NET_HTTP_CLIENT
    default y
    help
      https:// for the HTTP client: mbedTLS cut down to a TLS 1.2 client
      (ECDHE with AES-GCM; RSA and ECDSA certificates; include/
      mbedtls_config.h), verified against an embedded set of roots
      (src/net/ca_roots.h: Google Trust Services R1-R4, GlobalSign R1,
      ISRG X1/X2). A session takes ~35 KB of the Lua arena at its peak (a
      16 KiB receive record buffer is unavoidable: servers ignore
      max_fragment_length) and is freed when the request ends. Costs about
      70 KB of flash.

config NET_SNTP
    bool "SNTP wall-clock time (sys.time)"
    default y
    help
      lwIP's SNTP client polling pool.ntp.org (or cfg/ntp.json's "server")
      hourly. Gives Lua sys.time() and lets TLS check certificate dates.

config NET_WIREGUARD
    bool "WireGuard tunnel (an additional route, never the only one)"
    default n
    help
      A WireGuard client, so the device can be reached from outside the
      local network. The tunnel netif is never made lwIP's default route:
      USB-NCM and WiFi keep working whatever the tunnel is doing, because a
      remote-access path that can strand the device is worse than none.

      Keys and the peer endpoint come from cfg/wireguard.json on LittleFS,
      which is git-ignored and which the HTTP filesystem routes refuse to
      serve. Nothing is compiled in.

      Costs about 25 KB of flash (the implementation plus ChaCha20-Poly1305,
      BLAKE2s and X25519) and a few KB of RAM for one peer.

config NET_ETH_W5500
    bool "Ethernet: WIZnet W5500 on SPI (an optional second uplink)"
    default n
    select SPI_BUS
    help
      A W5500 in MACRAW mode as lwIP netif "e0": lwIP does ARP, IP and the
      DHCP client; the chip only moves frames. Optional in the same way WiFi
      is -- a build without it carries none of its code. The SPI traffic
      goes through src/io/spibus.c (semaphore-claimed bus, DMA in the
      background, completion from the main loop), so nothing ever waits on
      the chip. A board without the module fitted reports "not present" and
      touches nothing else.

      Costs about 12 KB of flash and ~1 KB of static RAM; frames in flight
      live in lwIP's own pools.

if NET_ETH_W5500

config ETH_W5500_SPI
    int "SPI block (0 or 1)"
    range 0 1
    default 1

config ETH_W5500_SCK
    int "SCK pin"
    default 14

config ETH_W5500_MOSI
    int "MOSI (TX) pin"
    default 15

config ETH_W5500_MISO
    int "MISO (RX) pin"
    default 8

config ETH_W5500_CS
    int "CS pin (driven as a plain GPIO)"
    default 13

config ETH_W5500_INT
    int "INT pin (-1 = none, poll only)"
    default 10

config ETH_W5500_RST
    int "RST pin (-1 = none, soft reset through MR)"
    default 11

config ETH_W5500_BAUD
    int "SPI clock (Hz)"
    default 30000000

config ETH_W5500_MAC_FILTER
    bool "Hardware MAC filter (own MAC + broadcast + multicast only)"
    default y
    help
      Sn_MR.MFEN. Off makes the socket promiscuous: harmless on a switched
      LAN, but every flooded unicast frame then crosses the SPI bus.

endif

config NET_UPLINK
    bool
    default y if NET_WIFI || NET_ETH_W5500
    help
      Uplink health and selection (src/net/uplink.c): the ONLY code that
      calls netif_set_default(). Pings the WireGuard endpoint on every
      interface with a gateway and moves the tunnel and new connections to
      the best one.

config NET_FORWARD
    bool "Forwarding rules from Lua (TCP proxy + UDP relay)"
    default y
    depends on LUA_ENABLE
    help
      net.forward{} from a script: a listen port relayed to another port on
      this device or to a host on any interface. Not IP forwarding: the
      gateway terminates and re-originates, so replies come back on the
      same pair whatever the interfaces. Rules belong to the script that
      made them.

config LWIP_MEM_SIZE_KB
    int "lwIP heap (KB)"
    default 48
    help
      lwIP's transmit heap. 48 KB suits RP2350's 520 KB SRAM; drop to 32 on an
      RP2040. Every other lwIP setting lives in include/lwipopts.h and must be
      changed there, never as a -D: those are unconditional #defines and win
      silently.

endmenu
